Nadia: FYI users reported a runtime error when calling the SDK from Next middleware on Vercel Edge: "Can't read property 'forEach' of undefined" coming from our header merge util.
Theo: seems like Headers in Next middleware behaves differently; it's iterable but some runtimes give a Headers object with no forEach. Which SDK version?
Nadia: starter pulled redwood-js-sdk@0.8.4. Stack trace points to lib/headers.js line where we call `orig.forEach((v,k) => set(k,v))`.
Ravi: simple fix is to normalize headers defensively. Suggest a small util: ```js
export function normalizeHeaders(h) {
  if (!h) return {}
  if (typeof h.entries === 'function') return Object.fromEntries(h.entries())
  if (typeof h[Symbol.iterator] === 'function') return Object.fromEntries(h)
  return {...h} // plain object
}
```
Theo: + also need to avoid mutating the original Headers instance in edge. Always create a fresh Headers or plain object and return that.
Nadia: another root cause: our package exposed node-only retry dependencies at top-level. Vercel's bundler included undici and stream polyfills and bundle exploded. Users see build errors like "Can't resolve 'stream'".
Ravi: we should add conditional exports and an explicit ./edge subpath. Example package.json exports: ```json
"exports": {
  "./edge": "./dist/edge.mjs",
  "./node": "./dist/node.cjs",
  "./": {
    "node": "./dist/node.cjs",
    "default": "./dist/edge.mjs"
  }
}
```
Theo: and move any node-only imports behind dynamic imports so static analyzers don't pull them in: `if (IS_NODE) { const mod = await import('./node-retry.js'); }`
Nadia: typing headaches too. Next projects compiling for edge only often don't want Node types. Proposed split: dist/types/index.d.ts (dom-friendly) and dist/types/node.d.ts. Add typesVersions or explicit types exports so TS picks the right one.
Ravi: short-term workaround for customers: import the edge entry directly in middleware: `import { createClient } from 'redwood-js-sdk/edge'` and clone headers manually: ```ts
const headers = Object.fromEntries(req.headers)
const client = createClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY })
await client.generate({ prompt: 'hi', headers })
```
Theo: PR I pushed: header normalization, merge util, conditional exports, dynamic node imports, split types, and README Edge notes + a migration snippet. I also added a tiny Next middleware fixture to CI that runs `next build` to catch these at PR time.
Nadia: great. CI-bot: CI started for branch fix/edge-normalize-headers-1827 440
Ravi: Docs-bot: once CI green, update starter README with two lines: recommended import for Edge and note about not initializing client at module top-level.
Theo: also published a canary: redwood-js-sdk@0.8.6-canary.1 with edge adapters. Please smoke-test on Vercel.
Nadia: testing now on our demo; header errors gone and build succeeds. Thanks all.
